4. PASSWORDS AND AUTHENTICATION
========================================================

Preserve the existing authentication system where possible.

Every user must be able to log in after the application is restarted
or redeployed, provided their account exists in the restored database.

Password information must be stored securely using an appropriate
password hashing algorithm such as Argon2id or bcrypt.

Never store plaintext passwords.

Never store recoverable plaintext passwords in backups.

If password hashes already exist, preserve them and maintain
backward compatibility.

Do not silently reset student passwords during migration or restore.

Persist account status, roles, and authentication-related records
that the application requires.

Review session and token behavior so that restoring a database does
not expose accounts or invalidate users unexpectedly.

Never log passwords, session tokens, or other authentication secrets.

