5. LESSON PROGRESS MUST SURVIVE ALL RESTARTS
========================================================

A student's progress must not depend on browser memory or a running
application process.

When a student completes a lesson:

1. Validate the action on the backend.
2. Write the completion record to SQLite.
3. Persist the completion timestamp.
4. Update the relevant progress information.
5. Update EXP or achievements where applicable.
6. Return success only after the database transaction commits.

When a student submits a quiz or homework assignment, persist the
submission, result, timestamp, and related records.

When the student logs in again, reload progress from SQLite.

When the browser is refreshed, progress must remain unchanged.

When the application process restarts, progress must remain unchanged.

When the Docker container is recreated, progress must remain unchanged.

When a previous valid database backup is restored, the portal must
display the progress contained in that backup.

Do not derive permanent progress exclusively from localStorage.

Browser storage may be used for temporary UI preferences or caching,
but SQLite must remain authoritative for persistent student progress.

Prevent lesson progress from being accidentally reset when lesson
content or frontend components are updated.

Use stable course, lesson, and student IDs instead of relying only
on lesson titles or array positions.

